.TH ENBAN 1 "2026-11-11" "enban" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
enban \- write, read, build and verify CD, DVD and Blu-ray discs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B enban
.RB [ \-\-drive
.IR drive ]
.RB [ \-\-theme
.IR name ]
.br
.B enban info
.BI \-\-drive " drive"
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B enban write
.I image
.BI \-\-drive " drive"
.RB [ \-\-speed
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-verify ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-eject ]
.br
.B enban read
.BI \-\-drive " drive"
.I image
.RB [ \-\-no\-verify ]
.br
.B enban verify
.BI \-\-drive " drive"
.RI [ image ]
.br
.B enban build
.I folder image
.RB [ \-\-fs
.IR list ]
.RB [ \-\-label
.IR name ]
.br
.B enban erase
.BI \-\-drive " drive"
.RB [ \-\-full ]
.br
.B enban checksum
.I image
.br
.B enban checksum
.BI \-\-drive " drive"
.br
.B enban queue
.I file
.RB [ \-\-no\-verify ]
.br
.B enban discovery
.BI \-\-drive " drive"
.RB [ \-\-sectors
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-\-speed
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-\-csv
.IR file ]
.br
.B enban doctor
.br
.B enban version
.br
.B enban help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B enban
opens a full-screen app on a terminal. It draws in the terminal's alternate
screen, reads keys itself and gives the screen back when it ends, on
.B q
or on an interrupt. Tab and the digits switch tabs, the arrows move between
the jobs on Home and Enter opens one, and
.B ?
shows the keys. The right pane describes the disc in the chosen drive.
On Write, Enter edits a field, space turns verify or eject on or off,
.B w
writes after the drive's name is typed, and
.B c
cancels a running write. On Read, Enter names the new image file, space
turns verify on or off,
.B r
reads the disc into it, and
.B c
cancels the read. On Verify, space turns the image comparison on or off,
Enter names the image,
.B v
verifies the disc, and
.B c
cancels the verify. On Build,
.B a
and
.B f
add a file or a folder to the disc layout,
.B x
and
.B n
remove or rename the focused entry, Enter edits the type, label, image or
target,
.B b
builds into the image or, after the drive's name is typed, onto the disc, and
.B c
cancels the build. The right pane then shows the file systems, the label,
the checks and the detected content instead of the disc. On Tools, Enter opens
the focused tool: Erase disc, where space picks quick or full and Enter erases
after the drive's name is typed, Tray, Capabilities and Checksum; Escape goes
back to the list. On Queue,
.B a
adds an image,
.B x
removes the focused one,
.B J
and
.B K
move it down or up, Enter sets its speed and copies,
.B v
turns the verify on or off,
.B o
saves the queue to a file and
.B s
starts it after each drive's name is typed. On Discovery, Enter sets the
sectors or the speed and
.B t
starts the test after the drive's name is typed; the tab draws the read rate
of the last run and compares the last five runs.
Without a terminal the app never opens.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B info
Describe the drive and the disc in it: vendor, product and revision, the disc
profile, whether the disc is blank, appendable or closed, its sessions, the
free sectors, the write speeds the drive offers and the kinds of disc it
handles. On Linux it reads the drive
through
.BR xorriso .
Reading a drive on macOS is not supported yet, so info stops there.
.TP
.B write
Burn
.I image
to the blank disc in the drive. The image must be a whole number of 2048-byte
sectors and fit the free space. enban asks for the drive's name, as in
.BR sr0 ,
and writes only when it is typed. Then it reads the disc back, compares it
with the image and ejects the disc. It refuses a disc that is not blank.
.TP
.B read
Copy the readable sectors of the disc into
.IR image ,
read the disc again to compare, and print the SHA-256 of the copy. It refuses
a disc that holds no data and never overwrites a file.
.TP
.B verify
Read every sector of a disc that holds data and, when
.I image
is given, compare the disc with it. Prints how many sectors were readable,
how many differ and how many could not be read, and exits non-zero naming the
first sector that differs or cannot be read.
.TP
.B build
Make a new
.I image
from the files in
.IR folder ,
with ISO9660 always and Joliet and UDF unless
.B \-\-fs
leaves them out. macOS builds with hdiutil; Linux builds with xorriso, and
with genisoimage when UDF is asked for. The volume name is the folder's, or
.BR \-\-label ,
in the letters ISO9660 allows. It never overwrites a file.
.TP
.B erase
Blank a rewritable disc that holds data: CD-RW, DVD-RW, DVD+RW, DVD-RAM or
BD-RE. It asks for the drive's name first and touches nothing when the
answer differs. A quick erase clears the start of the disc;
.B \-\-full
overwrites every sector.
.TP
.B checksum
Read
.I image
or the readable sectors of the disc in the drive once, and print their MD5,
SHA-1 and SHA-256.
.TP
.B queue
Write the images
.I file
lists, one per line as an image, a drive, then optionally a speed or
.B auto
and a number of copies, separated by tabs or spaces. Lines starting with # are
skipped. It asks for each drive's name once, writes each drive's images in
order while the drives work at the same time, waits for a blank disc before
each copy, verifies it unless
.B \-\-no\-verify
is given, and ejects it. A table at the end gives the state of every image.
.TP
.B discovery
Fill a blank disc with a known pattern, all its free sectors unless
.B \-\-sectors
says fewer, after the drive's name is typed. Then read it back in a hundred
stretches, compare every sector, and save the read rate of each stretch as CSV.
It names the first sector that reads back wrong or cannot be read.
.TP
.B doctor
List the tools enban needs and whether each is installed. Exits non-zero only
when a required tool is missing.
.TP
.B version
Print the version and the platform.
.TP
.B help
Print the usage page.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-drive " drive"
The drive info, write, read, verify, erase and the app use, such as
.IR /dev/sr0 .
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the result of info as one JSON object, for scripts.
.TP
.BI \-\-speed " n"
Write at
.I n
times the base speed of the disc, such as 8. By default the drive picks.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-verify
Write, read or queue without the second pass that compares the disc and the
image.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-eject
Leave the disc in the drive when the write ends.
.TP
.B \-\-verify
Compare the disc after write, read or queue, the default unless the settings
file turns it off.
.TP
.B \-\-eject
Open the tray when the write ends, the default unless the settings file turns
it off.
.TP
.BI \-\-fs " list"
The file systems build writes, from iso9660, joliet and udf, separated by
commas; iso9660 is required. All three by default.
.TP
.BI \-\-label " name"
The volume name build writes, up to 32 characters. The folder's name by
default.
.TP
.B \-\-full
Erase every sector of the disc instead of only its start.
.TP
.BI \-\-sectors " n"
How many sectors discovery writes; all free sectors by default.
.TP
.BI \-\-csv " file"
Where discovery saves the read rate; a new file under
.B ENBAN_DATA
by default.
.TP
.BI \-\-theme " name"
Colours of the terminal interface:
.BR kodougu ,
.BR catppuccin\-mocha ,
.BR catppuccin\-latte ,
.BR dracula ,
.BR nord ,
.BR gruvbox ,
.B tokyo\-night
or
.BR mono .
The default is
.BR kodougu .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ENBAN_CONFIG
The folder that holds the settings file,
.I ~/.config/enban
by default.
.TP
.B ENBAN_DRIVE
The drive when
.B \-\-drive
is not given.
.TP
.B ENBAN_DATA
The folder where discovery keeps its CSV files and its list of runs,
.I ~/.local/share/enban
by default.
.TP
.B ENBAN_QUEUE_WAIT
How many seconds queue waits for a blank disc before it gives up on an image,
600 by default.
.TP
.B ENBAN_THEME
The theme when
.B \-\-theme
is not given.
.TP
.B KODOUGU_THEME
The theme of every kodougu tool, when neither
.B \-\-theme
nor
.B ENBAN_THEME
is set.
.TP
.B KODOUGU_ACCENT
A colour such as #CBA6F7 that replaces the accent of the theme.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Turns colour off in every theme.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ENBAN_CONFIG/settings
Defaults, one
.I name=value
per line:
.BR write_verify ,
.B write_eject
and
.B read_verify
take yes or no,
.B write_speed
auto or a number,
.B build_fs
a list such as iso9660,joliet,
.B queue_wait
seconds and
.B theme
a theme name. Lines starting with # are skipped, and a line enban cannot use
is named in a warning. Options, then ENBAN_ variables, win over the file.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on any failure or refusal.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR drutil (1),
.BR hdiutil (1),
.BR xorriso (1)
